1
00:00:03,004 --> 00:00:07,442
This is Turno. Heat-safe shift planning for outdoor crews.

2
00:00:07,892 --> 00:00:12,698
Built for the AMD Developer Hackathon, Act three.

3
00:00:14,598 --> 00:00:22,582
Every summer, Italian regions ban outdoor work from half past twelve to four, on days the heat is rated high.

4
00:00:23,032 --> 00:00:29,680
A site supervisor still has to decide the rest of the day, hour by hour, and prove the ban was kept.

5
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:38,599
Milan, the thirty-first of July. Heavy work, a crew new to the heat.

6
00:00:39,049 --> 00:00:44,990
Turno planned this day the day before, one hour of safe work in a thirteen-hour day.

7
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:52,399
And then it checked itself against what actually happened. It allowed too much work in one hour. It says so.

8
00:00:56,004 --> 00:01:00,569
The strip is the whole day. Each column is an hour.

9
00:01:01,019 --> 00:01:11,223
Height is the minutes of work allowed. Colour is the heat. Above it, the forecast spread, and the black line is what really happened.

10
00:01:15,355 --> 00:01:24,316
Nine o'clock, hm. The forecast ran a little cool, so the plan allowed fifteen minutes, and reality allowed none.

11
00:01:24,766 --> 00:01:31,587
Turno marks it as too high rather than hiding it. A plan you can audit is worth more than one that looks perfect.

12
00:01:33,487 --> 00:01:38,614
Now the supervisor confirms the official rating for the day. High.

13
00:01:39,064 --> 00:01:46,298
The law takes over the afternoon. Those hatched blocks are the ban, and no forecast can change them.

14
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:54,181
Two o'clock is banned by the regional ordinance, quoted from its own text.

15
00:01:56,081 --> 00:02:02,452
Before the rating is confirmed, those same hours belong to the supervisor.

16
00:02:02,902 --> 00:02:11,124
Turno never guesses the official rating. It assumes the ban, and hands the hour to a person, with the reason.

17
00:02:15,030 --> 00:02:21,137
A model running on AMD explains the hour, in Italian and in English.

18
00:02:21,587 --> 00:02:29,302
Every number it writes is checked against the plan before it is shown. If one is not in the plan, the text is dropped.

19
00:02:31,202 --> 00:02:34,115
Gemma four writes the crew briefing from the plan.

20
00:02:34,565 --> 00:02:41,499
Same rule. The labels for each hour are fixed by the rules, and every time and number is checked.

21
00:02:45,106 --> 00:02:51,139
Regions rewrite these ordinances every summer. This is where AMD does its heaviest work.

22
00:02:51,589 --> 00:03:00,120
MinerU reads the region's signed PDF. DeepSeek proposes every field with a quote copied from the text.

23
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:05,618
This re-read ran on AMD just now, while you watched.

24
00:03:06,068 --> 00:03:14,721
Code accepts a field only if its quote is really in the document and contains the value. The rest goes to a person.

25
00:03:16,621 --> 00:03:23,933
Tomorrow's plan comes from the latest ECMWF ensemble run, the same forecast, the same rules.

26
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:33,014
The dates and the predictions were written down before the test days were scored.

27
00:03:33,464 --> 00:03:42,943
On ten summer days the ensemble and the rules missed one percent of heat-restricted hours, against six point six for a single forecast.

28
00:03:46,442 --> 00:03:52,954
The models read and explain. The rules decide. The supervisor has the last word.

29
00:03:53,404 --> 00:03:58,095
The code, the sources and every number are open.
